<blockquote>
<p>In other words, it's 'the greater good' theory, but in a way the reader understands the greater good on an immediate level, while also being aware of the sacrifice.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>a) I think that's rather disgusting, as that's not actually how the greater good should be considered&#8230; for example the wanton sacrifice of individuals within a nation to &quot;avoid spending 12 percent of it's GDP on imports.&quot; Not sure how dicey of a moral dilemma you've created here as its on its face, abominable.<br />
b) this is has sorta been done alot on the wiki as well.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The 'God Eater' thing is part of the narrative. It was originally built with the intention to kill (&quot;Eat&quot;) gods. With that in mind, it was designed to contain an entity with great power and convert it into raw unthinking energy. It just so happens that it's current users aren't aware of it's designed purpose and utilize it differently.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><em>ok, but my point is that you need to thematically connect the anomaly to the &quot;narrative&quot;. For example, if something broke down matter to create energy, I wouldn't assume that was used for killing gods. Typically we don't consider gods to simply be beings of matter. So what I am suggesting is you tweak this anomaly such that it is clear from the anomaly itself that there is something in it that is meant to kill the divine.</em></p>
<hr /></div>
</div>
</div>
<p>As someone fairly interesting in deicide as a narrative device, I am not sure what more you're trying to say. So the thing was meant to kill gods&#8230; ok? What thematic core to this anomaly/narrative are you trying to talk about? If its just a thing that which should kill a god&#8230; without more, I am not sure how intriguing that will be (not to mention there's plenty of that on the wiki too).</p>
<p>What I think you need to do is stop worrying so much about the plot and start thinking about some themes you could examine through this piece. What are you trying to talk about to the reader?</p>
<p>If its just &quot;<em>this crazy thing exists and oh isn't it a dilemma with a government using it this way</em>&quot; then I <span style="text-decoration: underline;">still have two problems</span>:<br />
1) the narrative does not seem connected to the anomaly. Why does it kill gods at all? Why would going into the lore of the object connect thematically to the discussion of a callous government willing to sacrifice human lives to save on annual budgets for imports?</p>
<p>2) As it stands, this mostly seems concerned with the discussion of the anomalous properties and the spooky implications of a callous government&#8230; but I think to stand out, it needs more. I also think the anomaly itself could be tweaked to differentiate itself from that thing that eats a life and makes energy (you can argue all you want, but <strong>why couldn't the government put in ANY organic matter to have the same effect?</strong> There's <span style="text-decoration: underline;">nothing inherently more potent in a human organism</span> than others concerning the conversion of mass into energy.)</p>

							</div>

								
					
